<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06TK">Remark <span data-tag="06TK">7.6.1.12</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $\{  X_ i \} _{i \in I}$ be a collection of objects of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ which admit a product $\prod _{i \in I} X_ i$. If each of the objects $X_ i$ is $n$-truncated (for some fixed integer $n$), then $X$ is also $n$-truncated (see Corollary <a data-tag="06TJ" href="/tag/06TJ">7.3.8.7</a>). In particular, if each of the objects $X_ i$ is subterminal, then $X$ is also subterminal. In this case, the isomorphism class $[X]$ is a greatest lower bound for the collection $\{  [X_ i] \} _{i \in I}$ in the partially ordered set $\operatorname{Sub}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ of Notation <a data-tag="04V9" href="/tag/04V9">4.7.4.9</a>. </p>
</article>
